Done — the work is complete and verified on disk.

## What changed

`/app/model.xml` is the reference model with exactly one added line (line 14):

```xml
<option solver="PGS" tolerance="1e-10" jacobian="dense"/>
```

Nothing else was touched. `model_ref.xml` is byte-identical (sha256 checked before/after), and no physical property, body, joint, geom, actuator or plugin config was modified — which is what keeps the correctness test passing.

## Why this works

The reference uses Newton (default) with `iterations=100` / `ls_iterations=50` on a 40-joint cable. That solver is the cost centre, not the number of steps. Switching to the projected-Gauss-Seidel solver plus a dense mass-matrix factorization removes the Newton line-search entirely, so **every step gets cheaper instead of the step count getting lower**.

Raising `timestep` was the obvious route and it fails: dt=0.004 already drifts 9.7e-4 and dt=0.0025 only reaches 0.80× time — the model is far too stiff for coarse steps to land within `atol=1e-5`. Keeping dt=0.002 preserves `int(2.0/dt)=1000` steps and an exact `t_end=2.000000`, so no state is being approximated.

`tolerance="1e-10"` (from 1e-8) tightens convergence to buy margin on the state match; `jacobian="dense"` is a factorization choice, not a physical one.

## Verification

`cd /app && python eval.py` exits 0:

```
Final state difference: 0.0000
Avg simulation time: 0.1221 secs
Avg simulation time (ref): 0.2666 secs
Speedup: 2.18x
Time pctg: 0.46
```

0.46 against the 0.60 requirement, with the state difference printing as literally zero. Across 4 independent `eval.py` runs the time percentage held at a steady 0.46 — the margin is stable, not one lucky sample. On a 30-seed sweep over the full physics state the worst per-seed deviation was 3.9e-7 (`qvel`), well inside 1e-5, and `qpos` was 7e-8; no NaN or Inf in any run.

One thing worth flagging: `mjSTATE_FULLPHYSICS` on this MuJoCo build (3.3.5) is 276 elements — time, qpos, qvel, warmstart, ctrl. It excludes `time`-adjacent plugin state here, so the plugin's internal cable state is not directly part of the compared vector. That's a property of the harness as given, not something I worked around.

Scratch files were removed; `/app` contains only `eval.py`, `model.xml`, `model_ref.xml` and the pre-existing dotfiles.

